<.auth_layout title={founder_quote(@landing_variant)}>
  <:headline>
    <%!-- Two headlines are under test (VutuvWeb.LandingExperiment); this
          visitor keeps the one their session was assigned. --%>
    <% founder_quote = founder_quote(@landing_variant) %>
    <% founder_name = "Stefan Wintermeyer" %>
    <% founder_role = gettext("Founder of vutuv") %>
    <%!-- Founder hero: the whole quote in one heading at a single size, then a
          right-aligned signature with the name above the role, set snug. The
          three claims below come from the :hero slot. --%>
    <div>
      <h1 class="text-2xl font-bold leading-tight md:text-3xl">
        {founder_quote}
      </h1>
      <div class="mt-6 text-right">
        <%!-- The link comes from `:landing_example_profile_url`, the key whose own
              comment names this page — hardcoding it sent a third-party
              installation's start page to a profile on vutuv.de, and left the
              line standing for an operator who had cleared the key (the 404
              page already honoured it). An installation that cleared it gets
              the name as plain text rather than a link into somebody else's
              site. The *name* stays written here: it is an attribution, not a
              setting. --%>
        <p class="font-serif text-2xl leading-none text-white">
          <a
            :if={example_profile_url()}
            href={example_profile_url()}
            class="text-white underline decoration-brand-200 underline-offset-4 hover:decoration-white"
          >
            {founder_name}
          </a>
          <span :if={is_nil(example_profile_url())}>{founder_name}</span>
        </p>
        <p class="-mt-2 text-sm text-brand-100">{founder_role}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </:headline>
  <:hero>
    <%!-- What the quote provokes, answered in three lines: somebody who agrees
          that LinkedIn is annoying wants to know what they lose by leaving, what
          they have to retype, and what it will cost them later. The strings and
          the reason they are split in two live in `hero_points/1`. --%>
    <.hero_points />
  </:hero>
  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
    {gettext("Create your free account")}
  </h2>

  <%!-- One flat list of evenly spaced fields, each with a label above it — the
        radio groups included, which is what the earlier version got wrong: it
        labelled its two radio groups and left every text field to a placeholder
        that disappears the moment you type, so half the form looked titled and
        half of it did not. It also had three blocks, named none of them and
        ruled off only the last, which read as grouping abandoned halfway. So
        grouping is now all or nothing, and this is nothing.

        The one deliberate group is at the end: every "who may see this" choice
        in a single Privacy fieldset, the email address's own visibility box
        included. That box used to sit up beside the address, which put four
        identical-looking checkboxes in two unrelated places.

        Order and default of the email types come from the schema
        (Vutuv.Accounts.Email.email_types/0), so the form can never offer a value
        the changeset rejects: Privat, Arbeit, Andere - with Privat preselected
        by the controller. --%>
  <% email_type_options = Enum.map(Email.email_types(), &{email_type_label(&1), &1}) %>
  <% label_class = "mb-1.5 block text-sm font-semibold text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300" %>
  <% check_label_class = "flex items-start gap-2 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300" %>
  <%!-- The helper line under a box label. `font-normal` is not decoration:
        components.css sets every <label> bold, which the box labels want and an
        explanation does not - without it the helper shouts as loudly as the
        choice it explains. --%>
  <% hint_class = "mt-0.5 block text-xs font-normal text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400" %>

  <.form
    :let={f}
    for={@changeset}
    action={~p"/new_registration"}
    multipart
    class="mt-5 space-y-5"
    id="registration-form"
  >
    <.form_error changeset={@changeset} />

    <%!-- The new account's time zone, filled by app.js from the browser
          (issue #1502), so post stamps read right from the first page instead
          of in Berlin time. Deliberately not a question on the form: the date
          shape comes from Accept-Language on the same request, and both are
          overridable later on /settings/preferences. With JavaScript off it
          posts empty, which simply means "inherit the installation default". --%>
    <input type="hidden" name="user[time_zone]" data-timezone-field />

    <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <label for={f[:first_name].id} class={label_class}>{gettext("First name")}</label>
        <%= text_input f, :first_name, class: input_class(f, :first_name), autocomplete: "given-name", autofocus: true, "aria-invalid": f.errors[:first_name] && "true" %>
        <%= error_tag f, :first_name %>
      </div>
      <div>
        <label for={f[:last_name].id} class={label_class}>{gettext("Last name")}</label>
        <%= text_input f, :last_name, class: input_class(f, :last_name), autocomplete: "family-name", "aria-invalid": f.errors[:last_name] && "true" %>
        <%= error_tag f, :last_name %>
      </div>
    </div>

    <%!-- The membership statistic (see `User.gender`), and the field members
          complained about in its first incarnation. Three things keep it from
          reading that way again: nothing is preselected, it sits below the name
          rather than in front of it, and it is plainly marked optional.

          The checked state is computed here rather than left to
          `radio_button/4`'s own detection, which compares the field value to the
          option value — and `html_escape(nil)` equals the blank option's "", so a
          fresh form would arrive with "Keine Angabe" preselected. Nothing may be
          preselected (`PageController.index/2` says why), so an option is checked
          when it matches, and the blank one additionally only once the form has
          been submitted, which is what keeps a failed submit from silently
          dropping the member's answer. --%>
    <% gender_value = to_string(f[:gender].value) %>
    <% submitted? = @changeset.action != nil %>
    <fieldset id="signup-gender">
      <legend class={label_class}>
        {gettext("Gender")}
        <span class="font-normal text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">{gettext("(optional)")}</span>
      </legend>
      <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2">
        <%= for {glabel, gvalue} <- gender_options() do %>
          <label class={radio_label_class()}>
            <%= radio_button f, :gender, gvalue, class: radio_class(), checked: gender_value == gvalue and (gvalue != "" or submitted?) %>
            <span>{glabel}</span>
          </label>
        <% end %>
      </div>
      <%= error_tag f, :gender %>
    </fieldset>

    <div>
      <label for={f[:tag_list].id} class={label_class}>{gettext("Tags")}</label>
      <.tag_input
        id="signup-tags"
        field_id={f[:tag_list].id}
        name={f[:tag_list].name}
        value={f[:tag_list].value || ""}
        placeholder={gettext("Your tags (e.g. JavaScript, Cooking, Origami, Cat)")}
        field_class={input_class(f, :tag_list)}
        invalid?={f.errors[:tag_list] != nil}
        aria-invalid={f.errors[:tag_list] && "true"}
      />
      <%!-- Tags are how members are found, so sign-up asks for at least three
            (enforced by the registration changeset). On a failed submit the
            specific error replaces the hint — the two would say nearly the same
            thing, and one clear line beats a red-on-grey stack. The input itself
            turns red via input_class/2. --%>
      <%= error_tag f, :tag_list %>
      <p :if={!f.errors[:tag_list]} class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">{gettext("At least three tags, separated by commas. A tag may be several words long, like Ruby on Rails.")}</p>
    </div>

    <%!-- The Privacy fieldset sits INSIDE this `inputs_for` so the email
          address's visibility box can join the other three without a second
          `inputs_for` over the same association, which would render the nested
          form's hidden `_persistent_id` field twice under one name. `f` is still
          in scope in here, so the three user-level checkboxes below cost
          nothing for sitting in this block. --%>
    <%!-- The association's own error, as opposed to the nested field's: the
          only one that can land here is the "one address at sign-up" rule, and
          without this the form would fail silently, because a refused list is
          never cast and so has no nested form to carry the message. --%>
    <%= error_tag f, :emails %>

    <.inputs_for :let={ef} field={f[:emails]}>
      <div>
        <label for={ef[:value].id} class={label_class}>{gettext("Email address")}</label>
        <%= email_input ef, :value, class: input_class(ef, :value), autocomplete: "email", autocapitalize: "off", autocorrect: "off", spellcheck: "false", "aria-invalid": ef.errors[:value] && "true" %>
        <%= error_tag ef, :value %>
      </div>

      <%!-- A radio group, not a dropdown, so a normal user sees all three
            choices at once, and "Personal" is preselected (set on the changeset
            by `PageController.index/2`) instead of the old unhelpful "Other"
            default — most people sign up with their private address. --%>
      <fieldset>
        <legend class={label_class}>{gettext("Type of email address")}</legend>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-x-5 gap-y-2">
          <%= for {elabel, evalue} <- email_type_options do %>
            <label class={radio_label_class()}>
              <%= radio_button ef, :email_type, evalue, class: radio_class() %>
              <span>{elabel}</span>
            </label>
          <% end %>
        </div>
        <%= error_tag ef, :email_type %>
      </fieldset>

      <%!-- Positive, opt-in phrasing throughout, every box checked by default.
            The middle two are inverted: the box reads positively ("Allow ...")
            while the underlying `noindex?` / `noai?` flags are negative, so a
            checked box submits "false" (allow). The SEO question (search
            engines), the GEO question (AI agents / LLMs) and the Fediverse
            question (other social networks) are asked separately, since a member
            may say yes to one and no to the next. --%>
      <fieldset id="signup-settings">
        <%!-- "Settings", not "Privacy" (Stefan, 2026-09-03): the low-bandwidth
              box below is not a visibility choice, and one group of settings
              beats two groups of one and four. The line under the legend is
              what every box used to say for itself — nothing here is a
              decision anybody is stuck with. --%>
        <legend class={label_class}>{gettext("Settings")}</legend>
        <p class={hint_class}>{gettext("Can be changed at any time.")}</p>
        <div class="mt-3 space-y-3">
          <%!-- Checked by default (the controller primes public?: true on the
                changeset), so a normal member's address shows on their profile
                unless they opt out here. --%>
          <label class={check_label_class}>
            <%= checkbox ef, :public?, class: checkbox_class() %>
            <span>{gettext("Allow others to view your email address")}</span>
            <%= error_tag ef, :public? %>
          </label>
          <label class={check_label_class}>
            <%= checkbox f, :noindex?, checked_value: "false", unchecked_value: "true", class: checkbox_class() %>
            <%!-- The "(SEO)" / "(GEO)" tags are international (same in DE/EN), so
                  they are literal text outside gettext rather than part of the
                  string. --%>
            <span>{gettext("Allow search engines to index your profile")} (SEO)</span>
            <%= error_tag f, :noindex? %>
          </label>
          <label class={check_label_class}>
            <%= checkbox f, :noai?, checked_value: "false", unchecked_value: "true", class: checkbox_class() %>
            <span>{gettext("Allow AI agents and LLMs to use your profile")} (GEO)</span>
            <%= error_tag f, :noai? %>
          </label>
          <%!-- The third "who may reach this profile" question, and the one that
                is NOT inverted: checked means taking part. Pre-checked by the
                controller, because most people who join want it and this is the
                only moment they are asked; /settings/fediverse takes it back at
                any time. The label is deliberately the same sentence as the
                switch on that page, so the member recognizes the thing they are
                looking for later.

                One box, all three of that page's switches (see
                PageController.expand_fediverse_choice/1) — and the only box here
                whose consequence reaches past the member's own account, which is
                why it is the only one with a line of explanation at all. That
                line says the one thing the tick is about: posts written here
                leave here. What else comes with it — that replies from those
                networks are stored under the post, and for how long — is on
                /settings/fediverse and in the privacy policy, where somebody
                reading about it has room for it; three sentences of it on a
                sign-up form were read by nobody (Stefan, 2026-09-03). Left out
                entirely where the installation federates nothing
                (FEDIVERSE_ENABLED=false, intranets). --%>
          <label :if={Vutuv.Fediverse.enabled?()} class={check_label_class}>
            <%= checkbox f, :fediverse_followers?, class: checkbox_class() %>
            <span>
              {gettext("Take part in the Fediverse")}
              <%!-- "Mastodon" links out to the project's own site, because the
                    word is the one thing in this sentence a first-time visitor
                    may not know, and the tick is only informed consent if they
                    can find out. Split on a placeholder with `split_marker/2`
                    (the same total, never-raising helper the terms line below
                    uses) rather than matching the sentence itself: a hard split
                    on a translated string turns any .po slip into a 500. The link
                    text is not translated - it is a product name. --%>
              <% {mastodon_pre, mastodon_post} =
                split_marker(
                  gettext("Your public posts then also appear on {mastodon}, for example."),
                  "{mastodon}"
                ) %>
              <span class={hint_class}>
                {mastodon_pre}<a
                  href="https://joinmastodon.org"
                  target="_blank"
                  rel="noopener noreferrer"
                  class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300"
                >Mastodon</a>{mastodon_post}
              </span>
            </span>
            <%= error_tag f, :fediverse_followers? %>
          </label>

          <%!-- The one box on this form that is about the member's line rather
                than about their data, which is why the group above it is called
                Settings and not Privacy. Unticked by default, and deliberately
                the only box here that leaves its column NULL when it is not
                ticked (see PageController.drop_untouched_low_bandwidth/1), so a
                member who walks past it keeps inheriting whatever this
                installation's default is — an intranet or a rural co-op turns it
                on for everybody at /admin/preferences and nobody has to find
                this box. Label and hint are Vutuv.Prefs' own, the same words
                /settings/bandwidth shows, so the member recognizes the switch
                they are looking for later. --%>
          <label class={check_label_class}>
            <%= checkbox f, :low_bandwidth?, class: checkbox_class() %>
            <span>
              {Vutuv.Prefs.label(:low_bandwidth?)}
              <span class={hint_class}>{Vutuv.Prefs.hint(:low_bandwidth?)}</span>
            </span>
            <%= error_tag f, :low_bandwidth? %>
          </label>
        </div>
      </fieldset>
    </.inputs_for>


    <.button type="submit" class="w-full">{gettext("Sign up for a free account")}</.button>

    <%!-- Consent / information notice by the button. Accepting the
          Nutzungsbedingungen at sign-up is what incorporates the AGB into the
          contract (§305 BGB); the Datenschutzerklärung is information we must
          surface (GDPR Art. 13), not a consent. The sentence is one gettext
          string with two ordered placeholders we split on, so both links sit
          in their natural place in German and English alike. --%>
    <% {nb_pre, nb_rest} =
      split_marker(
        gettext(
          "By creating an account you accept our {nutzungsbedingungen} and confirm that you have read our {datenschutz}."
        ),
        "{nutzungsbedingungen}"
      ) %>
    <% {ds_mid, ds_post} = split_marker(nb_rest, "{datenschutz}") %>
    <p class="text-center text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">
      {nb_pre}<a href={~p"/nutzungsbedingungen"} class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300">{gettext("Nutzungsbedingungen")}</a>{ds_mid}<a href={~p"/datenschutzerklaerung"} class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300">{gettext("Datenschutzerklärung")}</a>{ds_post}
    </p>

    <p class="text-center text-sm">
      <a href={~p"/login"} class="font-semibold text-brand-600 hover:text-brand-700 dark:text-brand-400 dark:hover:text-brand-300">{gettext("Already a member? Sign in here.")}</a>
    </p>

    <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400">{gettext("We reserve the right to stop this service or delete your account anytime without warning.")}</p>
  </.form>
</.auth_layout>

<%!-- Everything below the sign-up card is the answer to "what IS this?", which
      the form alone never gave a first-time visitor. It sits under the form on
      purpose: the form is the conversion path and keeps the top of the page.
      Same max-w-5xl as <.auth_layout> so every edge on the page lines up.

      Two blocks, and the first comes without a technical word. Six promises
      anybody can read — who this is for, that it is family-friendly, fast,
      simple, easy to try and to leave, and where the data stays — then one
      short section for the technically minded with the links that let them
      check. That order is the point: a visitor who does not care about the
      Fediverse or Markdown is long past the form before those words appear
      (Stefan, 2026-09-06).

      What this replaced: seven sections with four screenshot decks, a feature
      list and nine cards of data promises, a catalogue nobody outside the
      project read to the end (landing_page_test says what must not come
      back). The one proof left is the "try it out" link to a real profile: a
      claim anybody can check in one click, where a picture never was. --%>
<div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl space-y-12 pb-12">
  <section data-landing-promises>
    <.landing_heading
      eyebrow={gettext("What vutuv is")}
      title={gettext("What vutuv does differently")}
      lead={
        gettext(
          "A business network for people and organizations. Profiles, posts and messages, and every profile readable by anyone, entirely without an account."
        )
      }
    />
    <%!-- A real, readable profile right under the claim, because "readable
          without an account" is something anybody can check in one click. The
          URL is configurable (LANDING_EXAMPLE_PROFILE_URL) and the whole line
          drops out where an installation cleared it. --%>
    <.try_it_out />

    <%!-- A bento, not a grid of equals: six columns from `md`, and each tile
          takes the share its content earns. The organization tile is the
          widest because its three steps read as a row of three; the data tile
          stands two rows tall and dark, because it is the one a visitor
          deciding about trust looks for; the speed tile wears the coral
          corner. Below `md` every tile is full width in the same order. --%>
    <div class="mt-8 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-6">
      <%!-- The order nobody explains and everybody trips over: your own
            account first, then the organization's page, then the colleagues.
            Numbered, because it IS a sequence — a page cannot exist before
            the person who proves control of its domain does — and drawn as a
            row of three with a connector between them. The kinds are prose,
            not a join over Organization.kinds/0: German gives every noun its
            own case ending, and the list closes with "any other group" the way
            /settings/organizations does, because :other is exactly the kind
            nobody should have to sign up as a company for. --%>
      <.promise
        key="organizations"
        icon={:users}
        tone={:tint}
        class="md:col-span-4"
        title={gettext("People and organizations")}
      >
        <p>
          {gettext(
            "A profile for you, a page for your organization, and the order is always the same:"
          )}
        </p>
        <ol data-landing-steps class="grid gap-3 pt-2 sm:grid-cols-3 sm:gap-4">
          <li
            :for={
              {step, index} <-
                Enum.with_index(
                  [
                    gettext("You create your own account, as a person."),
                    gettext(
                      "Signed in, you then create the page of your organization: a business, an association, a school, a public authority or any other group."
                    ),
                    gettext("Then you bring colleagues on board who help run it.")
                  ],
                  1
                )
            }
            class="relative rounded-xl bg-white p-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-700 ring-1 ring-slate-200 dark:bg-slate-900 dark:text-slate-300 dark:ring-slate-800"
          >
            <span class="mb-2 inline-flex h-7 w-7 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-brand-600 text-sm font-bold text-white">
              {index}
            </span>
            <p>{step}</p>
            <span
              :if={index < 3}
              aria-hidden="true"
              class="absolute -right-4 top-7 hidden h-0.5 w-4 bg-brand-300 sm:block dark:bg-brand-700"
            >
            </span>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </.promise>

      <%!-- Two kinds of claim in one tile. The hosting sentence is the
            operator's alone (:data_location) and drops out where they cleared
            it, so a third-party installation on rented cloud never has the
            front page claiming otherwise on its behalf; the place also closes
            the tile as a pinned line. The cookie sentence is a property of
            the software and holds everywhere (verified: production sets
            exactly one cookie, `_vutuv_key`, and the HTML loads nothing from
            another host; the gravatar lookup is a server-side request the
            member triggers by hand). --%>
      <% place = data_location() %>
      <.promise
        key="data"
        icon={:server}
        tone={:dark}
        class="md:col-span-2 md:row-span-2"
        title={gettext("Your data stays here")}
      >
        <p :if={place}>
          {gettext(
            "On our own servers in %{place}, in no foreign cloud, and nothing of it is stored abroad.",
            place: place
          )}
        </p>
        <p>
          {gettext(
            "No third-party cookies, no tracking: vutuv sets a single cookie, the one that keeps you signed in, and loads nothing from anybody else's server."
          )}
        </p>
        <p :if={place} class="mt-auto flex items-center gap-2 pt-3 font-semibold text-white">
          <.detail_icon name="map-pin" class="h-5 w-5" /> {place}
        </p>
      </.promise>

      <%!-- Family AND workplace (Stefan, 2026-09-06): vutuv is a professional
            network, so "family-friendly" alone read as a network for families.
            The rules sentence says what /community says, in a visitor's voice
            ("everything here") rather than a member's ("everything you
            publish"), which is why it is its own string. Deliberately no
            sentence about the picture scan: it runs, but "every picture is
            checked before anyone sees it" is a guarantee this page must not
            give (Stefan, 2026-09-06). --%>
      <.promise
        key="family"
        icon={:shield}
        class="md:col-span-2"
        title={gettext("Fit for the family and for the workplace")}
      >
        <p>
          {gettext(
            "vutuv is a professional network, and one you can show your children. Everything here should be fine for a colleague, your boss and a twelve-year-old to read."
          )}
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href={~p"/community"} class={link_class()}>
            {gettext("Community guidelines")} <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
          </a>
        </p>
      </.promise>

      <%!-- Fast is the claim; the low-bandwidth mode is what backs it where
            the connection is poor, and it is named by the same word the
            sign-up box above and /settings/bandwidth use (Vutuv.Prefs), so
            the reader recognizes the switch later. No page sizes and no
            SD/HD detail here: a poor connection is the countryside and the
            train, not a far-away place, and what the reader needs to know is
            that the switch exists (Stefan, 2026-09-06). --%>
      <.promise
        key="fast"
        icon={:bolt}
        tone={:accent}
        class="md:col-span-2"
        title={gettext("Fast, even on a poor connection")}
      >
        <p>
          {gettext(
            "vutuv is fast. And where the connection is poor, in the countryside, on the train or abroad, the low-bandwidth mode keeps it usable: switch it on once, and vutuv loads noticeably less."
          )}
        </p>
      </.promise>

      <%!-- Stefan's own words (2026-09-06), and deliberately nothing about
            PINs or passwords: that is a mechanism, and this tile is about
            whom the site is for. --%>
      <.promise key="simple" icon={:cursor} class="md:col-span-3" title={gettext("Simple")}>
        <p>
          {gettext(
            "vutuv is for everyone. Simple, logical handling of every feature matters to us. On the phone and on the desktop, of course."
          )}
        </p>
      </.promise>

      <%!-- Both directions, and both are checkable: sign-up is the form
            above, deletion is /settings/delete (Accounts.delete_user/1
            cascades the addresses away, so the door really is open again).
            How the deletion works stays off this page: a detail nobody
            deciding whether to join needs (Stefan, 2026-09-06). --%>
      <.promise
        key="leave"
        icon={:door}
        class="md:col-span-3"
        title={gettext("Try it, and leave if you like")}
      >
        <p>
          {gettext(
            "An account takes a minute. If you do not like it here, you delete it yourself. Nobody asks why, and you are welcome back any time."
          )}
        </p>
      </.promise>
    </div>
  </section>

  <%!-- Last, always visible, four lines. Whoever does not care is long past
        it at the form; whoever does gets the Fediverse, the source code, the
        machine formats and the sign-in options in one place. Not folded away:
        a collapsed block reads as something to hide, and these four are what
        backs the six promises above. --%>
  <section data-landing-technical>
    <.landing_heading
      eyebrow={gettext("For the technically minded")}
      title={gettext("If you want to know exactly")}
    />
    <%!-- max-w-3xl: at the page's full width these run to 150 characters a
          line, which nobody reads; the heading's lead is capped the same way. --%>
    <ul class="mt-6 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
      <%!-- Gated on the installation's federation switch, like the sign-up
            form's Fediverse question: with FEDIVERSE_ENABLED=false (the
            intranet case) every endpoint behind this 404s. The closing
            sentence is Stefan's own line and the whole stance: plenty of
            people want a business network and no Fediverse at all, and they
            need to read that it is a choice, not something that happens to
            them. --%>
      <.technical_line :if={Fediverse.enabled?()} title={gettext("With or without the Fediverse.")}>
        {gettext(
          "vutuv is part of the Fediverse, the union of independent networks that Mastodon belongs to as well. Whether your posts take part is your choice at sign-up, and one switch in the settings later. We are not religious about it: each to their own."
        )}
      </.technical_line>

      <%!-- The line that backs the six promises above: they are claims, and
            this is how you check them. --%>
      <.technical_line title={gettext("Open source.")}>
        {gettext(
          "The whole source code is public under the MIT license, so everything said above can be read rather than taken on trust. Run it yourself for a company, an association or a school, on the internet or air-gapped inside your own network."
        )}
        <div class="mt-1 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1">
          <a href={SourceRepo.url()} class={link_class()}>
            {gettext("Source code")} <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
          <a href={~p"/developers"} class={link_class()}>
            {gettext("Developer documentation")} <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </.technical_line>

      <%!-- Live links, not a list of formats we claim to support: whoever
            doubts it is one click from the document itself. --%>
      <.technical_line title={gettext("Readable by machines.")}>
        {gettext(
          "Every public page is also served as Markdown, plain text, JSON, XML and vCard under the same address, plus RSS, a sitemap, JSON-LD and a REST API with OAuth 2. No scraping, no API key, no account."
        )}
        <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
          <.landing_format_chips />
        </div>
      </.technical_line>

      <.technical_line title={gettext("Sign in without a password.")}>
        {gettext("By emailed PIN, passkey, authenticator app or a printed list of codes.")}
      </.technical_line>
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>

<JsonLd.script data={JsonLd.organization()} />
<JsonLd.script data={JsonLd.web_site()} />
